import { serialize } from "node:v8"

pnpm i sequelize 
pnpm i mysql2
pnpx sequelize-cli init 

create migration file : 
pnpx sequelize-cli migration:generate --name create-hotel-rool 
pnpm run migrate



functional requirements : 
list hotels / filter hotels by city / filter hotels by price range / filter hotels by rating / sort hotels by price / sort hotels by rating / search hotels by name
make reservations 
see details of restrau

non functional requirements :
no double booking 
concurrent booking 
peak hotel seraching > peak hotel booking ( read heavy > write heavy )

eg : 10k hotels -> each hotel has 100 rooms -> 1 million rooms
1b -> MAU ,  DAU=> 1%  of MAU   -> 10 million DAU -> 100k concurrent users 

10M dau => atleast 10 queries per user 
total search  queries => 10M * 10 => 100 million queries per second
search query per sec (qps) -> 100m/10^5 => 1000 qps
10^5 is seconds approx in a day ( 24*60*60 = 86400 ~ 10^5)
peak load -> 10x peak -> 10^4 (qps) request 

write requests 
50% hotels are booked -> 2 night stay 
0.5M bookings per day /2 => 250k write requests per day
peak load 2x of it => 500k write requests per day => 5 qps booking 

//the system expects 250,000 write requests per day, not because of “every 2 days”, but because each booking spans 2 nights.



//idempotency booking methods : 
Client-generated idempotency keys (most common)
Request deduplication by payload hash
Server-generated idempotency token
Idempotency via unique constraints / primary keys
Combination of time window + client ID

In-memory Cache (Redis, Memcached)
Database (Persistent Storage)
Hybrid Approaches (e.g., Redis + Database)

locks :
problems : 
dirty read  (reading uncommitted data)
non repeatable read (data changes between reads)
phantom read (new rows appear in subsequent reads)

Enforcing ACID properties 


SQL mechanisms : 
isolatioin levels :
read uncommitted (allows dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads)
read committed( prevents dirty reads but allows non-repeatable reads and phantom reads)
repeatable read (prevents dirty reads and non-repeatable reads but allows phantom reads)
serializable (prevents dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads)

pessimistic locking :
SELECT ... FOR UPDATE (locks selected rows until transaction completes) (read committed + lock on row (prevting update))


optimistic locking : 
versioning (using a version number or timestamp to detect concurrent modifications)
